/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Emotions & Relationships (batch z) */
WB.L("anger-and-venting", "emotions", "beginner", "Anger: why letting off steam backfires",
  "Punch a pillow, scream into the void, smash plates in a rage room. It feels like it should help. The research says it usually makes things worse.",
  [
    ["What anger is for", "Anger is an emotion built to confront perceived wrongs, and the trouble lies in what we do with it.",
      "Anger has a bad reputation, but it exists for a reason. It flares when we feel wronged, blocked or treated unfairly, and it prepares the body to push back. Heart rate and blood pressure rise, adrenaline surges, muscles tense and attention narrows onto the problem. In our evolutionary past that energy helped people defend food, territory or family.\n\nToday it can still be useful. Anger signals that something matters, it can give us courage to stand up for ourselves or others, and it has fuelled many campaigns against injustice. The trouble is not feeling anger. It is what we do next, and one of the most popular answers is wrong."],
    ["The steam engine theory", "The idea that anger builds up like pressure and must be released is old and intuitive, but largely mistaken.",
      "Aristotle wrote about catharsis, the purging of emotions, when he described how audiences felt after watching tragedy. Centuries later Sigmund Freud and others pictured emotions as fluid or steam in a closed system. Bottle it up and the pressure grows until it bursts out somewhere harmful. Let it out safely and you return to calm.\n\nThis hydraulic picture shaped twentieth century advice: hit a punching bag, scream into a pillow, play violent games to get aggression out of your system. It lives on in rage rooms, where customers pay to smash crockery and old televisions. The picture feels right because anger feels like pressure. But feelings are not steam, and the brain is not a kettle."],
    ["The punching bag test", "People who hit a punching bag while thinking about their insulter became more aggressive, while those who sat quietly were calmest.",
      "In 2002 psychologist Brad Bushman tested catharsis on about 600 university students. Each wrote an essay and received harsh feedback, supposedly from another student, including that it was one of the worst essays the reader had seen. One group then hit a punching bag while thinking about the person who insulted them. A second hit the bag while thinking about getting fit. A third sat quietly for a couple of minutes.\n\nAfterwards everyone could blast their insulter with unpleasant noise through headphones. The group who vented while focused on their anger felt the most anger and were the most aggressive. The quiet group were the least angry and least aggressive. Doing nothing beat letting off steam."],
    ["Why venting feeds anger", "Venting is practice: it keeps the body aroused and the grievance fresh, so calming down works better.",
      "Venting fails because it is rehearsal. Replaying an insult, ranting about it or acting out hitting someone keeps the alarm system switched on and the offence at the front of your mind. Psychologists call the mental version rumination, one of the strongest predictors of anger lasting longer and spilling onto innocent targets. A revved-up body is also primed to read new frustrations as further attacks.\n\nIn 2021 Sophie Kjærvik and Brad Bushman pooled more than 150 studies. Activities that lower arousal, such as slow breathing, relaxation, mindfulness, yoga and simply pausing, reliably reduced anger. Hitting things, and in some studies vigorous exercise straight after a provocation, generally did not. Regular exercise is still good for mood and health. When you are boiling, though, cool the body first: breathe out longer than you breathe in, step away, or delay your reply."],
    ["Using anger well", "The goal is neither suppressing nor venting anger but expressing it clearly, without aggression, once you have calmed down.",
      "Stuffing anger down has costs too, including strain on relationships and health. The skill lies between exploding and bottling. Once the first surge settles, ask what the anger points to. Was a boundary crossed? Is something unfair? Then say so plainly, focusing on the behaviour and its effect rather than attacking the person. 'I was embarrassed when the mistake was raised in front of everyone' invites a conversation. 'You always humiliate me' invites a fight.\n\nTalking helps only when it moves towards understanding or solving the problem. Anger that is understood can lead to useful change. Anger that is merely released usually leads to more anger.\n\nIf anger often arrives too fast and hard, harms relationships, or has been linked to violence, see a GP for a referral. In immediate danger, call 000."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the main problem with anger according to this lesson?", ["Feeling it at all, since it has no useful purpose","What we do with it, because the feeling can be useful","Its effect on blood pressure, which is always harmful","Its link to evolution, which makes it outdated"], 1, "Anger signals that something matters; the harm comes from what we do with it."],
    [1, "What does the 'steam engine' theory of anger claim?", ["Anger fades fastest when you ignore it completely","Anger is caused by heat and fatigue in the body","Anger builds like pressure and must be released","Anger is learned only by watching violence"], 2, "The theory treats anger as pressure in a closed system, but feelings do not work like steam."],
    [2, "In the punching bag study, which group ended up least angry and least aggressive?", ["Those who hit the bag while thinking about getting fit","Those who hit the bag while thinking about their insulter","Those who wrote a second essay","Those who sat quietly for a couple of minutes"], 3, "Venting while focused on the anger made things worse; doing nothing beat letting off steam."],
    [3, "What should you do first when you are boiling with anger?", ["Calm your body by breathing slowly or stepping away","Hit something safe to burn off the energy","Write a long message to the person immediately","Go for a sprint straight after the argument"], 0, "Calming activities reduce anger, while arousing ones usually do not help in the moment."],
    [4, "What is the healthiest way to deal with anger once it settles?", ["Pretend it is not there and wait for it to fade","Calmly say what happened and how it affected you","Rant with friends who will take your side","Wait for the person to apologise first"], 1, "Clear, specific expression of the behaviour and its effect invites conversation rather than a fight."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Doing nothing beat hitting a punching bag. Why venting backfires."});

WB.L("why-we-cry", "emotions", "beginner", "Why we cry",
  "Humans are, as far as scientists can tell, the only animals that shed tears because they feel something. So why did evolution give us such a strange and visible signal?",
  [
    ["Three kinds of tears", "Your eyes make basal tears all the time, reflex tears for irritants and emotional tears when feelings run high.",
      "Your eyes are never truly dry. Small glands keep them coated with basal tears, which wash away dust, feed the cornea and contain proteins that help fight infection. Every blink spreads a fresh layer.\n\nReflex tears flood in when something irritates the eye, such as smoke, grit or a chopped onion. Cutting an onion releases a volatile sulphur compound that irritates nerves in the eye, so the eye flushes it away.\n\nThe third type, emotional tears, are the mysterious ones. Nothing touches the eye. Sadness, grief, frustration, relief, joy or being deeply moved triggers them. Humans are, as far as scientists can tell, the only animals that clearly cry for emotional reasons, and thinkers have puzzled over why for centuries."],
    ["The plumbing", "Tears are made by glands above each eye and drain into your nose, which is why crying makes your nose run.",
      "The main tear-making organ is the lacrimal gland, tucked under the bony brow above the outer corner of each eye. Tiny ducts release fluid onto the eye, and blinking sweeps it towards the inner corner. There, two little openings called puncta, one on each eyelid, act like drains. They lead to a channel that empties into the nasal cavity.\n\nNormally this system handles the gentle flow of basal tears unnoticed. When you cry hard, tears are made faster than the drains can cope with, so they spill over the lower lid and down your cheeks. The extra fluid pouring into your nose explains the sniffing and runny nose that come with a good cry."],
    ["A signal to others", "Darwin saw emotional tears as a by-product, but many researchers now see them as a visible signal that draws help.",
      "In his 1872 book The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals, Charles Darwin studied crying closely, watching his own children among others. He concluded that weeping was probably incidental, a result of muscles squeezing around the eyes during screaming, rather than useful in itself.\n\nMany later researchers disagree. Dutch psychologist Ad Vingerhoets, who has studied crying for decades, argues that tears are above all a signal to other people. Unlike a sob, which anyone nearby can hear, tears are visible mainly up close, to those we are most likely to trust. They say someone is hurting, overwhelmed or vulnerable, and they tend to draw comfort and help.\n\nTears may also signal no threat, since blurry eyes and a crumpled face are hard to fight with."],
    ["Tears change faces", "Visible tears make a face look sadder and more in need of support, which explains why tears are so moving.",
      "In 2009 psychologist Robert Provine and colleagues showed people photos of tearful faces, then the same photos with the tears digitally removed. With tears, the faces were rated clearly sad. Without them, many looked far less sad, and some were read as puzzled, concerned or neutral. Other studies find that people who see tearful faces feel more inclined to help or comfort.\n\nThis supports the signal idea. Adult tears tend to appear in moments of helplessness, and we can find them powerfully moving even in strangers on a screen.\n\nCrying changes across life, too. Newborns cry loudly from birth, often with few tears at first, because their tear system is still maturing. Adult triggers shift towards loss, conflict, powerlessness and being moved by kindness or reunion."],
    ["Does a good cry help?", "Crying helps most when you are comforted and understood, and the idea that tears flush out stress chemicals is poorly supported.",
      "Ask people whether crying helps and most say yes. Yet in laboratories, people watching sad films often feel a little worse straight afterwards. Some research suggests the lift arrives later, as feelings recover over the following hour or so.\n\nWhat matters most is what happens around the tears. People who are comforted or feel understood are more likely to say crying helped. Those who feel ashamed or judged often feel worse. A popular claim says tears flush stress chemicals from the body. Emotional tears differ slightly in make-up, but there is little evidence that so small a volume removes enough of anything to matter.\n\nCrying that rarely stops, or being unable to cry for a long time after a major loss, is worth raising with a GP."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What triggers emotional tears, unlike reflex tears?", ["Smoke, grit or onion vapour irritating the eye","Feelings such as grief, relief, joy or being moved","The blink that spreads fluid across the eye","Dry air that dries out the surface of the eye"], 1, "Reflex tears respond to irritants, while emotional tears come from feelings rather than anything touching the eye."],
    [1, "Why does crying hard make your nose run?", ["Tears drain through small openings into the nose","The sinuses swell in response to stress hormones","The nose makes extra mucus to replace lost fluid","Tears are made in glands inside the nose"], 0, "Tears drain through the puncta into the nose, and extra tears overload this system."],
    [2, "What do many researchers now think emotional tears are mainly for?", ["Washing dust from the eye after stress","Lowering blood pressure during distress","Signalling vulnerability and drawing comfort","Removing waste chemicals from the body"], 2, "Tears are visible mainly up close, so they work as a signal to people we trust."],
    [3, "What did Provine's photo experiment show when tears were digitally removed?", ["Faces were judged just as sad as before","Faces looked less sad, and sometimes neutral","Faces looked more distressed than before","Faces looked angrier to most viewers"], 1, "Tears make a face read as clearly sad and encourage others to help."],
    [4, "What best predicts whether people feel better after crying?", ["How many tears they shed","How long they spend crying in total","Whether they cried alone in a quiet room","Whether they felt comforted and understood"], 3, "Support around the tears matters most, while the chemical flushing claim has little evidence."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your eyes make three kinds of tears. Only one is still a puzzle."});

WB.L("laughter-science", "emotions", "beginner", "Laughter: it's not really about jokes",
  "We think we laugh because something is funny. One scientist spent years eavesdropping on laughing strangers and found that most laughter has nothing to do with humour at all.",
  [
    ["Laughter isn't about jokes", "Studies of everyday conversation found most laughter follows ordinary remarks, because it works as a social signal.",
      "In the 1990s neuroscientist Robert Provine, of the University of Maryland, Baltimore County, took a simple approach. Rather than showing people comedy in a lab, he and his students hung around shopping centres, campuses and city streets, noting what people had just said when someone laughed.\n\nAcross more than a thousand episodes, only around 10 to 20 per cent of laughs followed anything like a joke. Most came after lines such as 'I'll see you guys later' or 'Are you sure?' Speakers also laughed more than their listeners.\n\nLaughter, Provine concluded, is less a reaction to comedy than a social signal: a way of saying we are friendly, relaxed and on the same side."],
    ["Laughter is for company", "People laugh far more with others than alone, and laughter spreads, which is why laugh tracks work.",
      "If laughter were just about funny things, you would laugh as much alone as with friends. You don't. In diary studies, Provine found people laughed about 30 times more often in social situations than alone, and much of what they laughed at alone came from television or radio, which supply a kind of company.\n\nLaughter is also contagious. Hearing others laugh makes us more likely to laugh, which is why television producers began adding canned laughter in the 1950s, using a machine nicknamed the 'laff box', invented by sound engineer Charley Douglass. Most people say they dislike laugh tracks, yet studies have found they can make people laugh more at the same jokes."],
    ["Older than words", "Laughter grew out of the panting play sounds of our ape ancestors and seems to signal that rough play is just play.",
      "Laughter is far older than language. Chimpanzees, bonobos, gorillas and orangutans make breathy panting sounds when they play-wrestle or are tickled. Researchers who compared these sounds found a family tree of laughter that matches the apes' evolutionary relationships. Ape laughter pants in and out with each breath, while human laughter chops a single outward breath into rhythmic 'ha-ha-ha' bursts.\n\nThe roots may go deeper. In the late 1990s neuroscientist Jaak Panksepp found that rats make high-pitched chirps, too high for human ears, when tickled, and tickled rats chase the researcher's hand for more. In mammals, laughter seems to have begun as a signal that rough play is only play."],
    ["Real and fake", "Listeners can often tell spontaneous laughs and smiles from deliberate ones, but neither is a foolproof lie detector.",
      "Neuroscientist Sophie Scott, of University College London, separates spontaneous laughter, which bursts out of us, from volitional laughter, the polite laugh we produce on purpose. Brain imaging shows partly different areas respond to each, and people can usually tell them apart. In a 2014 study by Gregory Bryant and Athena Aktipis, sped-up spontaneous laughs began to sound like animal calls, while deliberate ones still sounded human. That suggests real laughter relies on an older vocal system than speech does. Deliberate laughter is not dishonest; it is often a kind, useful signal.\n\nSmiles work similarly. Guillaume Duchenne, a nineteenth century French neurologist, noted that real joy also tightens the muscle around the eye, creating crow's feet. But many people can fake this, so it is not a foolproof test."],
    ["Good company, not medicine", "Shared laughter builds closeness and eases pain, but claims that laughter cures disease go beyond the evidence.",
      "Social laughter does something useful. In 2012 a team led by evolutionary psychologist Robin Dunbar found that people who laughed together at comedy videos could tolerate more pain than those who watched dull films. They linked this to the brain's own opioid-like chemicals. Shared laughter helps people feel closer, soothes tension and makes tough situations bearable, as anyone in a hard job with dark humour knows.\n\nBut claims that laughter cures disease, or that a set number of laughs replaces exercise, go far beyond the evidence.\n\nContagion can reach extremes. In 1962 at a girls' school in Kashasha, Tanganyika (now Tanzania), uncontrollable laughing spread to villages and affected roughly a thousand people over months. No infection or poison was found; it is described as mass psychogenic illness, a physical response spreading through a group under stress."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Provine's street observations find about what people laughed at?", ["Most laughs followed ordinary remarks, not jokes","Most laughs followed jokes told by strangers","Most laughs followed comedy on television","Most laughs followed teasing and insults"], 0, "Only about 10 to 20 per cent of laughs followed anything like a joke, so laughter is mainly a social signal."],
    [1, "Why does laughter in a group tend to increase?", ["People laugh to cover up boredom or awkwardness","Laughter is contagious, so it spreads between people","Groups tell funnier jokes than people alone do","Being alone makes people avoid laughing on purpose"], 1, "Laughter spreads between people, which is why we laugh far more in company and laugh tracks work."],
    [2, "What does the evidence about apes and tickled rats suggest laughter began as?", ["A warning cry about a nearby threat","A way of attracting a possible mate","A signal that rough play is friendly","An early form of simple speech"], 2, "Play sounds in apes and rats suggest laughter began as a signal that rough play is friendly."],
    [3, "What did sped-up recordings reveal about spontaneous laughs?", ["They sounded just as human as deliberate laughs","They sounded more like nonhuman animal calls","They became impossible to tell from speech","They sounded more polite and controlled"], 1, "Real laughter seems to rely on an older vocal system than the one we use for speech."],
    [4, "What is a fair claim about laughter and health?", ["Laughing a set number of times daily replaces exercise","Laughter cures many serious diseases","Shared laughter can raise pain tolerance and build closeness","Laughter has no social benefits at all"], 2, "Shared laughter has real social and short-term benefits, but wider health claims are exaggerated."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Most laughter follows ordinary remarks, not jokes. So what is it for?"});

WB.L("self-compassion", "emotions", "beginner", "Self-compassion: being on your own side",
  "Many of us talk to ourselves in ways we would never talk to a friend. Research suggests that harsh inner critic is not the motivator we think it is.",
  [
    ["The inner critic", "Constant self-attack makes people more anxious and less willing to learn from mistakes, rather than more motivated.",
      "Picture a friend who has just botched a job interview. You would probably say interviews are nerve-racking, one bad day does not define them, and they will do better next time. Now picture yourself in the same position. Many people's inner voice says something like 'You idiot, you always choke.'\n\nWe tend to believe this harshness keeps us sharp, and that going easy on ourselves would make us lazy. Psychologists who study self-compassion argue the opposite is often true. Constant self-attack tends to make people more anxious, more afraid of failure and less willing to look honestly at their mistakes, which makes learning from them harder."],
    ["Three parts of self-compassion", "Self-compassion means self-kindness, remembering common humanity and mindful awareness, and it differs from self-esteem.",
      "The modern science took off in 2003, when psychologist Kristin Neff defined self-compassion and built a way to measure it. It has three parts. Self-kindness means answering your own pain and failures with warmth, not harsh judgement. Common humanity means remembering that struggle and imperfection are part of being human. Mindfulness means noticing painful feelings clearly, without ignoring them or being swept away. In short, treat yourself in hard moments as a good friend or coach would.\n\nIt is not self-esteem. From the 1980s a self-esteem movement spread through schools, even a California task force in 1986. A major 2003 review led by Roy Baumeister found that boosting self-esteem did little for school or work results. Self-esteem often rests on judging yourself above average, so it collapses when you fail. Self-compassion holds steady either way."],
    ["Kindness that motivates", "Experiments suggest self-compassion makes people more willing to improve after failure, not less.",
      "In 2012 psychologists Juliana Breines and Serena Chen, at the University of California, Berkeley, tested whether self-compassion breeds complacency. Students sat a difficult vocabulary test designed so most would do poorly. Afterwards some were encouraged to be understanding towards themselves, some got a self-esteem boost telling them they must be intelligent, and others got no message.\n\nOffered the chance to study before a second test, the self-compassion group studied longer than either other group. In other studies, people prompted towards self-compassion were more willing to take responsibility for a past wrong and try not to repeat it. Kindness towards yourself seems to make facing your flaws feel safer."],
    ["What it is not", "Self-compassion is not self-pity or self-indulgence, and it works by calming the body's threat response.",
      "Critics worry that self-compassion is a polite word for excuses. It is not. Self-pity dwells on how unfair life is to me in particular, the opposite of common humanity. Self-indulgence grabs short-term comfort, like another night on the couch instead of training. A compassionate response is often the tougher one: the good coach who says 'that hurt, it's understandable, now let's work out what went wrong.' You can hold high standards and still speak to yourself decently.\n\nPsychologist Paul Gilbert describes a threat system, a drive system and a soothing system. Self-attack fires up the threat system, with racing heart, tension and urge to hide. Self-compassion is thought to engage the soothing system. Higher self-compassion is linked to lower anxiety and depression, though much of this research is correlational."],
    ["Practising it", "Simple habits, like talking to yourself as you would to a friend, build self-compassion over time.",
      "Self-compassion is a skill that improves with practice. One simple method is the friend test. When you notice a spiral of criticism, ask what you would say to a mate in the same situation, then say that to yourself, even if it feels awkward.\n\nNeff suggests a short self-compassion break in tough moments that runs through her three parts: acknowledge that this is hard, remind yourself that others struggle with this too, and offer yourself a kind word. Some people find a physical gesture helps, such as a hand on the chest. Writing a letter to yourself from the point of view of a kind, wise friend is another exercise used in research.\n\nNone of this replaces professional help when you need it, but it is a good daily habit."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do psychologists say about harsh self-criticism?", ["It keeps people sharp and motivated to improve","It tends to increase anxiety and fear of failure","It has little effect on how people learn","It helps most when the mistake is serious"], 1, "Constant self-attack tends to increase fear of failure and make honest reflection harder."],
    [1, "Why does self-esteem often fail when you need it most?", ["It rests on being above average, so failure topples it","It is built from other people's kindness, which fades","It is only available to people who succeed regularly","It needs hours of meditation to maintain"], 0, "Self-compassion does not depend on being special or successful, so it holds steady after failure."],
    [2, "In the Breines and Chen experiments, what did the self-compassion group do before the second test?", ["They skipped studying because they felt reassured","They studied for the same time as everyone else","They studied longer than the other two groups","They asked to be excused from the second test"], 2, "Self-compassion made students more willing to improve, not complacent."],
    [3, "Which description fits self-compassion rather than self-indulgence?", ["Choosing comfort tonight and training later","Dwelling on how unfair life has been to you","Telling yourself mistakes do not matter","Admitting the pain, then working out what to fix"], 3, "A compassionate response keeps high standards but speaks to yourself decently."],
    [4, "What is the 'friend test' for building self-compassion?", ["Say to yourself what you would say to a friend","Ask a friend to judge your performance","Compare yourself with your friends' results","Avoid the problem until a friend raises it"], 0, "Speaking to yourself as you would to a mate shifts the inner voice from attack to support."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Being kind to yourself after failure may make you try harder, not less."});

WB.L("love-languages", "emotions", "beginner", "Love languages: what the science says",
  "Words, time, gifts, help or touch: millions of couples have sorted themselves into love languages. Relationship scientists have now put the idea to the test.",
  [
    ["Where it came from", "Gary Chapman proposed in 1992 that people give and receive love in different ways, and couples flounder when they mismatch.",
      "Gary Chapman, a Baptist pastor and marriage counsellor in North Carolina, noticed a pattern. Partners often loved each other but felt unloved, because each expressed affection in a way the other did not register. In 1992 he published The Five Love Languages, arguing that people give and receive love through different channels.\n\nThe book became one of the best-selling relationship books ever, with versions for parents, workplaces and teenagers. Online quizzes let people find their primary language, and the terms have entered everyday speech.\n\nThe five are words of affirmation, quality time, receiving gifts, acts of service and physical touch. Chapman's central claim is that each person has one or two primary languages, and that speaking your partner's is the key to them feeling loved."],
    ["Putting it to the test", "Research finds little support for the idea that matching love languages makes couples happier.",
      "If Chapman were right, couples whose languages match should be noticeably more satisfied. Studies have tested this and the results are underwhelming. A 2017 study by Bunt and Hazelwood found that matching love languages did not predict relationship satisfaction, while partners' broader self-regulation did.\n\nPeople also often do not fit one primary language; many value all five quite highly. Questionnaires force artificial choices, such as asking whether you would rather receive a hug or a compliment, when most people would happily take both. Psychologists have also questioned whether five is the right number at all."],
    ["A balanced diet", "Love is better thought of as a balanced diet needing many nutrients than as a single language.",
      "In 2024 psychologists Emily Impett, Haeyoung Gideon Park and Amy Muise reviewed love languages against decades of relationship science. They found three core assumptions lacked strong evidence: that people have a single primary love language, that there are exactly five, and that matched couples are happier.\n\nRather than discarding the idea, they offered a different metaphor. Love is less like a language you either speak or do not, and more like a balanced diet. Most people need affection, attention, support, appreciation and closeness in some mix, and that mix can change over a lifetime or even week to week. A partner recovering from surgery may crave practical help; one facing a big exam may need encouragement."],
    ["What the idea gets right", "Treated as a conversation starter rather than a personality type, love languages can help you ask what your partner values.",
      "None of this makes love languages useless. The core insight, that people notice and value different gestures and that you should not simply give the love you would like to receive, is sound advice. Many couples find the vocabulary helps start conversations they might otherwise avoid, such as 'I feel closest to you when we spend time together, not when you buy me things.'\n\nThe risk comes when the label becomes a rule or an excuse. Someone says 'my language is gifts' and feels entitled to presents, or 'touch isn't my language' and dismisses a partner's need for affection. Used as a conversation starter instead of a type, the idea can do good."],
    ["What really matters", "Responsiveness, turning towards small bids for connection and showing appreciation predict happy couples better than any type.",
      "Relationship scientists point to patterns that matter more than categories. One is perceived partner responsiveness, studied by Harry Reis and others: the sense that your partner understands you, values you and cares for your needs.\n\nAnother comes from John Gottman's research, which describes everyday 'bids' for connection, such as pointing out a bird outside or sighing about a hard day. Couples who regularly turn towards these bids, responding with interest rather than ignoring them, tend to stay together and stay happier. Expressing gratitude, sharing good news and celebrating each other's wins also predict closeness.\n\nIn short, happy couples pay attention to each other's changing needs and keep showing up, whatever form the love takes."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was Chapman's central claim about love languages?", ["Everyone expresses and receives love in the same way","Each person has one or two primary love languages","Couples should avoid expressing love through gifts","Only physical touch creates lasting relationships"], 1, "Chapman argued that couples flounder when they speak different languages of love."],
    [1, "What did research find about couples whose love languages match?", ["They were far more satisfied than other couples","They were far less satisfied than other couples","Matching did not predict relationship satisfaction","They stayed together longer in every study"], 2, "Studies have found little support for the idea that matching love languages makes couples happier."],
    [2, "How did the 2024 review suggest thinking about love instead?", ["As a balanced diet of needs that changes over time","As a fixed personality type set in childhood","As one language that can be learned in a weekend","As a ranking of which partner loves more"], 0, "Most people need a mix of needs met, and the mix can shift even from week to week."],
    [3, "What is the best way to use the love languages idea?", ["As a rule that your partner is obliged to follow","As an excuse to ignore your partner's other needs","As a quiz result that fixes your personality for life","As a conversation starter about what each of you values"], 3, "The vocabulary helps start conversations, but turning it into a label can become a rule or an excuse."],
    [4, "What does the research say predicts happy couples better than love-language types?", ["Having the same hobbies and interests as your partner","Responding to small bids for connection, and appreciation","Avoiding all disagreements between partners","Giving your partner the most expensive gifts"], 1, "Responsiveness and turning towards bids matter more than any category."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Millions have a 'love language'. The research is less convinced."});

WB.L("regret-science", "emotions", "intermediate", "Regret: the things we didn't do",
  "In the short term we regret our mistakes. Over a lifetime, research suggests, the chances we never took weigh far more heavily.",
  [
    ["The emotion of 'if only'", "Regret comes from imagining a better alternative you feel responsible for, which is why it stings and why it is useful.",
      "Regret is a thinking emotion. To feel it, you must imagine an alternative version of events, the one where you spoke up, studied harder, stayed, left, or did not hit send, and compare it with reality. Psychologists call this counterfactual thinking. It sets regret apart from disappointment: you can be disappointed by the weather, but you regret things you feel responsible for.\n\nThat sense of responsibility is why regret stings, and also why it can be useful. In 2005 psychologists Neal Roese and Amy Summerville reviewed studies of what people regret most and found the top areas were education, career, romance and parenting. These are areas where choices are significant and change still feels possible."],
    ["Silver and bronze", "How we feel about an outcome depends heavily on what we imagine instead, as Olympic bronze medallists show.",
      "In a well-known 1995 study, psychologists Victoria Medvec, Scott Madey and Thomas Gilovich showed volunteers footage of medallists at the 1992 Barcelona Olympics and asked them to rate how happy each looked. Bronze medallists appeared happier than silver medallists, both just after their events and on the podium.\n\nThe researchers pointed to counterfactual thinking. Silver medallists compare upwards, with the gold they nearly won. Bronze medallists compare downwards, with finishing fourth and going home with nothing. Later studies often find similar patterns, though not always, since expectations matter: a silver can delight an athlete who never expected a medal. The lesson is broader than sport."],
    ["Why inaction haunts us", "Over a lifetime we regret what we did not do more than what we did, because missed chances stay open-ended.",
      "Gilovich and Medvec noticed a striking pattern. About a recent decision, people regret actions more: the rash comment or bad purchase stings. But looking back over a whole life, the pattern reverses and the biggest regrets are mostly things not done, such as not pursuing education, not taking a chance on a relationship, not spending time with family.\n\nThey suggested why. Regrets of action get repaired: we apologise, fix the problem or find a silver lining. Inactions are open-ended. A bad action has a known outcome, but a path not taken can be imagined endlessly, and it often turns out wonderfully in our heads. We also forget the real reasons we held back, such as money, family duties or genuine risk, so past caution looks like cowardice."],
    ["The regrets of the dying", "Bronnie Ware's observations of dying patients match the research on regrets of inaction, though they are not a scientific study.",
      "Australian palliative care worker Bronnie Ware spent several years caring for people in the last weeks of their lives. In 2009 she wrote a blog post about the regrets they shared, later expanded into a book. The most common was wishing they had had the courage to live a life true to themselves rather than the one others expected. Others included wishing they had not worked so hard, had expressed their feelings, had stayed in touch with friends and had let themselves be happier.\n\nHer list is personal observation rather than a scientific study, and should be read that way. Still, it lines up strikingly well with the research on regrets of inaction."],
    ["Four regrets, made useful", "Regrets fall into foundation, boldness, moral and connection types, and examined kindly they point to what you value.",
      "Writer Daniel Pink gathered regrets from thousands of people in many countries for his 2022 book The Power of Regret and sorted them into four types. Foundation regrets: failing to build a stable base, like not saving or not finishing study. Boldness regrets: chances not taken. Moral regrets: doing the wrong thing, such as cheating or betraying someone. Connection regrets: relationships allowed to drift.\n\nEach type shows something you deeply value. Handled well, regret is useful. Writing or talking about it shrinks it. Treating your past self kindly, remembering what you knew then, helps you learn rather than punish yourself. Imagining advice to a friend, or the view from ten years on, makes the lesson clearer. And when unsure, the bolder choice is often the one you will not regret."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What separates regret from simple disappointment?", ["It lasts much longer than disappointment","Imagining a better outcome you were responsible for","It only happens after a financial loss","It is felt only about other people's choices"], 1, "Regret depends on counterfactual thinking and a sense of responsibility."],
    [1, "Why did bronze medallists often look happier than silver medallists?", ["They trained harder and enjoyed the process more","They received larger prizes and sponsorships","They compared themselves with fourth place, not gold","They had lower expectations of every competition"], 2, "Our feelings depend on what we imagine instead: upward comparison hurts, downward comparison soothes."],
    [2, "Over a whole lifetime, which regrets tend to dominate?", ["Impulsive purchases and rash comments","Chances we never took or things left undone","Mistakes that were quickly fixed","Decisions made under pressure from others"], 1, "Action regrets get repaired or explained away, while missed chances remain open-ended and imagined as wonderful."],
    [3, "How should Bronnie Ware's list of dying regrets be understood?", ["As a controlled experiment with a large sample","As proof that working hard always causes regret","As a medical study from an Australian hospital","As personal observations, not a scientific study"], 3, "Her list is not scientific, but it lines up well with findings about regrets of inaction."],
    [4, "What does a 'boldness' regret involve?", ["A chance not taken, like a trip never made","Failing to save money or finish study","Betraying or cheating another person","Letting an old friendship drift apart"], 0, "Pink's four types are foundation, boldness, moral and connection regrets, and boldness regrets are missed chances."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Over a lifetime, which regrets weigh more: what we did, or didn't do?"});

WB.L("fear-science", "emotions", "intermediate", "Fear: how your brain keeps you alive",
  "A snake-shaped stick on a bush track can make you leap before you know why. Fear is fast, ancient and occasionally wrong, by design.",
  [
    ["The body's alarm", "Fear triggers a rapid, automatic set of bodily changes, including freezing, that prepare you to survive danger.",
      "When you sense a threat, your body changes in a fraction of a second. The sympathetic nervous system and adrenaline raise your heart rate and blood pressure and speed up breathing. Blood is diverted to large muscles and away from digestion, which is why fear can bring a churning stomach or dry mouth. Pupils widen, sugar is released for energy and sweating increases. Goosebumps are a leftover from ancestors whose fur stood on end to look bigger.\n\nThe phrase 'fight or flight' was popularised by physiologist Walter Cannon in the early twentieth century. Researchers now add freezing, since stillness makes animals harder to spot. Freezing is an involuntary reflex, not a choice or a sign of weakness, so people who froze in a crisis should not blame themselves."],
    ["The amygdala shortcut", "The amygdala can trigger defensive reactions before the cortex has worked out what the threat is.",
      "Deep in each temporal lobe sits an almond-shaped cluster of neurons called the amygdala, named from the Greek word for almond. Studying rats from the 1980s, neuroscientist Joseph LeDoux traced how threat signals reach it. A quick, rough 'low road' runs from the thalamus, a sensory relay station, straight to the amygdala. A slower 'high road' passes through the cortex, which analyses the scene in detail.\n\nThe low road lets you jump at a stick that looks like a snake before your cortex has decided it is a stick. Mistakes are cheap and missing a real snake is not. LeDoux has since stressed that the amygdala drives defensive reactions, not necessarily the conscious feeling of fear."],
    ["Woman without fear", "A patient with damage to both amygdalae showed little fear of snakes or haunted houses, yet panicked when breathing carbon dioxide.",
      "Some of the most striking evidence comes from a woman known in research as S.M. A rare genetic condition, Urbach-Wiethe disease, damaged the amygdala on both sides of her brain. In a 2011 study led by Justin Feinstein, she handled snakes at an exotic pet shop and had to be stopped from touching dangerous ones. At a haunted house attraction she laughed at the performers and even startled one. She also struggled to recognise fear in other people's faces.\n\nThere is a twist. In a later study, breathing air enriched with carbon dioxide, which creates a feeling of suffocation, brought on a panic attack. Threats from inside the body, it seems, can trigger fear without the amygdala."],
    ["Learned and prepared fears", "Evolution may prepare us to learn some fears quickly, but which fears we develop depends heavily on experience.",
      "Why do so many people fear snakes and spiders, which rarely harm anyone in cities, but not cars or power points, which kill far more? Psychologist Martin Seligman proposed in 1971 that evolution has 'prepared' us to learn certain fears quickly.\n\nExperiments by Susan Mineka and colleagues supported this. Laboratory-raised rhesus monkeys showed little fear of snakes at first, but after watching videos of other monkeys reacting fearfully to snakes, they quickly developed the fear. When the videos were edited so the monkeys seemed to fear flowers, the watchers did not learn to fear flowers.\n\nHumans also pick up fears by watching others, especially parents. Some fears may be prepared, but which ones a person develops depends heavily on experience."],
    ["Anxiety, phobias and thrills", "Fear answers a present threat and anxiety an uncertain future one; both are normal, and treatable when they get out of proportion.",
      "Fear responds to a specific, present danger, such as a car swerving towards you, and fades once the threat passes. Anxiety is a drawn-out apprehension about things that might happen, like a medical result or next week's interview. Both are useful in moderation; mild anxiety can sharpen preparation. Problems arise when they are out of proportion to the danger, last long or interfere with life. Anxiety disorders are among the most common mental health conditions, and effective treatments exist, especially exposure-based therapy, which helps people safely face what they fear. A GP is a good first step.\n\nMany people also enjoy fear when they know they are safe. Researchers at Aarhus University's Recreational Fear Lab, studying visitors to a haunted house, found enjoyment peaked at moderate levels of fear."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why should someone who froze during a crisis not blame themselves?", ["Freezing is an involuntary reflex, not a weakness","Freezing only happens to people who are poorly trained","Freezing means the danger was never real","Freezing is a habit that can be easily unlearned"], 0, "Freezing is a normal reflex that makes animals harder to spot."],
    [1, "Why can you jump at a stick that looks like a snake?", ["The cortex always analyses the scene before the body reacts","A fast route to the amygdala reacts before the cortex checks","The eyes send a false signal when light is poor","Snakes release a scent that triggers the response"], 1, "The low road is fast but rough, because a false alarm is cheap and a missed snake is not."],
    [2, "What did patient S.M. reveal about fear?", ["Fear cannot be produced by the body in any way","Fear depends on seeing a threat with your eyes","Fear is entirely learned from watching parents","Body alarms can cause fear without the amygdala"], 3, "S.M. felt little fear of snakes yet panicked when breathing carbon dioxide."],
    [3, "What did the monkey experiments suggest about fears of snakes versus flowers?", ["Monkeys never learn fears by watching others","Monkeys learned to fear both about equally","Monkeys learned fear of snakes, not of flowers","Monkeys learned fear of flowers more easily"], 2, "Evolution may prepare us to learn some fears quickly, but experience still shapes which ones we develop."],
    [4, "What best distinguishes anxiety from fear?", ["Anxiety is always a disorder, but fear never is","Anxiety concerns a possible threat; fear a present one","Anxiety is caused by genes, while fear is learned","Anxiety fades within a few seconds, unlike fear"], 1, "Fear fades once the threat passes, while anxiety is drawn out and about what might happen."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why your body can jump before your brain knows what it saw."});

WB.L("power-of-touch", "emotions", "intermediate", "The power of touch",
  "Your skin has nerve fibres that seem to exist mainly to register a caring stroke. Touch is one of the first languages we learn, and one we never stop needing.",
  [
    ["The first sense", "Touch develops first and is central to how babies bond, grow and survive, as kangaroo care shows.",
      "Touch is the first sense to develop in the womb, and for a newborn it is a primary way of experiencing the world. Being held, rocked and stroked helps regulate a baby's temperature, breathing and heart rate, and calms distress. In the 1950s Harry Harlow's infant rhesus monkeys clung to a soft cloth mother rather than a wire one that provided milk. The experiments are now considered cruel, but they helped overturn the idea that babies attach to carers simply because carers feed them.\n\nIn 1978 in Bogotá, Colombia, paediatrician Edgar Rey Sanabria faced an incubator shortage and began having mothers hold premature babies skin-to-skin on their chests. Called kangaroo mother care, it can improve survival, warmth, breathing, feeding and weight gain. The World Health Organization recommends it, and skin-to-skin contact is common in Australian hospitals."],
    ["Nerves made for caress", "Special nerve fibres in hairy skin respond best to slow, gentle stroking, giving us a dedicated channel for affectionate touch.",
      "Skin is packed with sensors for pressure, vibration, temperature and pain. In the late twentieth century, Scandinavian researchers including Åke Vallbo and Håkan Olausson studied a slow-conducting nerve fibre in human hairy skin, such as the arm and back, called C-tactile afferents. These respond most strongly to gentle stroking at roughly the speed of a caress, a few centimetres per second, and at about skin temperature.\n\nThey seem linked to the pleasant, emotional feeling of touch rather than to identifying textures or shapes. In one striking case, a patient who had lost the large nerve fibres that carry ordinary touch information could still faintly detect gentle stroking and report it as pleasant. Our bodies appear to have a dedicated channel for affectionate touch."],
    ["Touch speaks", "People can communicate distinct emotions through touch alone, and small touches can shift behaviour.",
      "In a 2006 study led by psychologist Matthew Hertenstein, pairs of strangers sat either side of a barrier, unable to see each other. One put an arm through, and the other tried to communicate specific emotions only by touching the forearm. Receivers identified anger, fear, disgust, love, gratitude and sympathy well above chance. Each had a typical touch: love was often stroking, sympathy patting, gratitude shaking hands, and anger hitting or squeezing.\n\nBrief touches can also shift behaviour. In a 1984 restaurant experiment by April Crusco and Christopher Wetzel, diners lightly touched on the hand or shoulder by a waitress tended to leave larger tips than those not touched."],
    ["Hugs and health", "Supportive touch is linked with a calmer stress response, though research cannot yet prove hugs themselves protect health.",
      "Touch from a trusted person can damp the body's stress response. In experiments where women faced the threat of a mild electric shock in a brain scanner, holding their husband's hand reduced activity in threat-related brain regions, and the effect was stronger in happier marriages.\n\nIn a 2015 study led by psychologist Sheldon Cohen, around 400 healthy adults reported how often they were hugged, then were deliberately exposed to a common cold virus and monitored in quarantine. Those who received more frequent hugs and had more social support tended to have less severe symptoms if they got sick. The study cannot prove hugs caused this, since huggers may differ in other ways, but it fits a wider pattern linking warm contact with health."],
    ["Culture, consent and need", "Good touch is always welcome touch, and many people who get little caring contact can find safe ways to add some.",
      "Norms around touch differ widely. In an often-quoted informal 1960s study, psychologist Sidney Jourard counted café couples touching in an hour: very high in San Juan and Paris, very low in Florida and none in London. It was small, but later research also finds big differences between countries and relationships. Whatever the culture, touch helps only when wanted. Unwanted touch triggers stress, and people, including children, may decline a hug. Asking 'Do you want a hug?' costs nothing.\n\nSome people, especially those who live alone, older people and the isolated, go weeks without kind touch. 'Touch starvation' is an everyday phrase, not a diagnosis, but surveys link lack of touch with loneliness. Welcome hugs, holding hands, massage, sport, dancing, patting a pet or a hand on the chest can all help."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Harlow's monkey experiments help overturn?", ["The idea that touch develops last of the senses","The idea that babies bond with carers only for food","The idea that premature babies need incubators","The idea that mothers are more important than fathers"], 1, "Infant monkeys preferred a soft cloth mother to a wire one with milk, showing that contact matters in its own right."],
    [1, "What are C-tactile afferents specialised to detect?", ["Sharp pain from cuts and burns on the skin","Slow, gentle stroking at about skin temperature","Heavy pressure from a firm grip or squeeze","Fast vibration from rough or textured objects"], 1, "These slow nerve fibres respond best to caress-like stroking and are linked to the pleasant feeling of touch."],
    [2, "What did Hertenstein's barrier experiment show?", ["Strangers cannot recognise emotions without seeing a face","Only love can be communicated by touch","Touch communicates emotions only between close family","People can convey distinct emotions through touch alone"], 3, "Receivers identified emotions such as love, gratitude and anger from forearm touch alone, well above chance."],
    [3, "Why can't the hug and cold study prove hugs protect health?", ["Nobody in the study was exposed to the virus","The adults were too young to catch colds","People who are hugged more may differ in other ways","Hugs were only counted during the quarantine"], 2, "The study shows a link, not cause, which still fits a wider pattern linking warm contact with health."],
    [4, "What is the key condition for touch to be beneficial?", ["That it lasts at least a full minute","That the other person is a total stranger","That it takes place somewhere private","That the person receiving it wants it"], 3, "Unwanted touch triggers stress rather than comfort, so asking first makes the gesture safer."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your skin has nerves tuned to a caress. Here's what they're for."});

WB.L("emotional-contagion", "emotions", "intermediate", "Emotional contagion: how moods spread",
  "One grumpy person can sour a whole meeting; one cheerful barista can lift a queue. Emotions pass between people almost like a cold, and mostly without our noticing.",
  [
    ["Catching feelings", "Emotional contagion is the automatic tendency to pick up others' emotions, and it begins in infancy.",
      "You walk into a tense room and feel your shoulders tighten before anyone speaks. You chat with an excited friend and leave in a better mood. Psychologists call this emotional contagion. The term was popularised by Elaine Hatfield, John Cacioppo and Richard Rapson in their 1994 book Emotional Contagion.\n\nIt starts remarkably early. In studies from the 1970s, newborn babies only a day or two old were more likely to cry when they heard another baby crying than when they heard other noises of the same loudness. Long before we can talk, we are tuned in to the feelings of others."],
    ["Mimicry and feeling", "We automatically copy others' expressions, and this copying may nudge our own feelings, though the effect is modest.",
      "The main mechanism appears to be mimicry. In conversation we copy each other's expressions, tone, speech rhythm and posture, mostly unaware. Swedish psychologist Ulf Dimberg measured tiny electrical signals in facial muscles while people viewed faces. Smiling faces triggered slight activity in smiling muscles, and angry faces in frowning muscles, within a fraction of a second, even when faces were flashed too briefly to be consciously seen.\n\nCopying a face may nudge feeling through facial feedback. A famous 1988 pen-in-teeth study claimed smilers found cartoons funnier, but a 2016 replication across 17 labs found no effect. The 2022 Many Smiles project found that mimicking or posing a smile could make people feel slightly happier, though the pen method did not reliably work. Facial feedback seems real, but modest."],
    ["Moods at work", "One person's mood can ripple through a team, and leaders' moods are especially contagious.",
      "In a 2002 study, organisational psychologist Sigal Barsade of the Yale School of Management had volunteers negotiate how to share a pool of money. Unknown to them, one member was a trained actor who behaved with cheerful enthusiasm, warm calm, irritable hostility or depressed sluggishness. The actor's mood spread to the others, and groups with a cheerful actor reported more cooperation and less conflict.\n\nOther research has found that leaders' moods are especially contagious, since people watch those in charge closely. This matters in offices, hospital wards and building sites, where a calm supervisor can steady a tense shift. Emotions are not just private states; they are part of a team's working conditions."],
    ["Networks and feeds", "Moods may spread through networks and online feeds, but the size of the effects is debated.",
      "In 2008 Nicholas Christakis and James Fowler analysed Framingham Heart Study data, which also recorded who was friends, neighbours or relatives. They reported that happiness was linked among friends, friends' friends and even friends' friends' friends. Critics noted that people befriend others like themselves and share environments such as suburb or job market, which can make happiness cluster without anything spreading. The size of these network effects is still debated.\n\nIn a 2014 Facebook and Cornell study of about 689,000 users, news feeds were quietly tweaked for one week in 2012 to show fewer positive or fewer negative posts. Users seeing fewer positive posts wrote slightly fewer positive words and more negative ones, and the reverse held too. Effects were tiny, but contagion seemed possible through text alone. The study sparked criticism because users had not knowingly agreed."],
    ["Guarding your climate", "Knowing about contagion lets you choose what you catch and what you pass on.",
      "Emotional contagion is not a flaw. It helps us connect, empathise and coordinate. But knowing about it gives you choices. Notice when your mood shifts after time with certain people, places or feeds, and ask whether the feeling is yours or you have simply caught it.\n\nPeople in caring and frontline roles, such as nurses, teachers, paramedics and police, are constantly exposed to others' distress and anger. Deliberate decompression after a shift, through exercise, a chat with a trusted mate or time outdoors, can stop it piling up.\n\nContagion also works in your favour. A calm voice can lower the temperature of a tense room, and spending time with steady, warm people is one of the simplest ways to borrow some of their steadiness."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the studies of newborn babies show?", ["Babies cried more at any loud noise","More often at another baby's cry than at other sounds","Babies only respond to their mother's voice","Babies cannot respond to emotions before six months"], 1, "Even a day-old baby responds to another baby's distress, so we are tuned to others' feelings from the start."],
    [1, "What is the main mechanism behind emotional contagion?", ["Mimicry of expressions, voices and posture","Reading other people's thoughts","Learning from explicit instruction","Sharing the same food and sleep patterns"], 0, "We copy others' faces and voices automatically, often within a fraction of a second."],
    [2, "What happened in Barsade's group negotiation study when the actor was cheerful?", ["Groups worked more slowly","The actor was quickly identified by the group","Groups reported more cooperation and less conflict","Nothing changed, as moods did not spread"], 2, "One person's mood spread to the others and shaped how well the team worked together."],
    [3, "Why do critics doubt that happiness 'spreads' through friendship networks?", ["The data came only from online posts","People befriend similar others and share surroundings","Happiness cannot be measured at all","The study excluded neighbours and relatives"], 1, "Similarity and shared surroundings can produce clusters without any contagion, so the size of the effect is debated."],
    [4, "How can people in frontline roles stop other people's distress piling up?", ["Push down every feeling until the shift is over","Decompress after a shift with exercise or time outdoors","Only ever work alongside calm and cheerful colleagues","Stop caring about the distress of the people they help"], 1, "Deliberate decompression and noticing where a mood came from help you choose what you carry."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Newborns cry when they hear other babies cry. Moods spread like this."});

WB.L("emotional-labour", "emotions", "advanced", "Emotional labour: when feelings are part of the job",
  "Flight attendants smile through turbulence; debt collectors scowl on cue. In 1983 a sociologist showed that many jobs sell not just our time and effort, but our feelings.",
  [
    ["Feelings for sale", "Emotional labour is managing your own feelings as part of paid work, so that customers feel a certain way.",
      "In 1983 sociologist Arlie Hochschild published The Managed Heart. Her idea: many jobs demand more than physical or mental effort. Workers must also produce particular emotions in themselves and, through them, in customers. She called this emotional labour. A nurse stays composed in front of frightened families. A funeral director offers quiet sympathy, day after day.\n\nHochschild studied Delta Air Lines flight attendants, who were trained to treat the cabin as their living room and to imagine a rude passenger might simply be frightened of flying. Debt collectors faced the opposite task: suppressing sympathy and projecting sternness. In both cases, feelings had become part of what the employer sells, and so something to train and monitor."],
    ["Surface and deep acting", "Workers can fake the expected emotion on the outside, or work to genuinely feel it, and sometimes it comes naturally.",
      "Hochschild borrowed two ideas from the theatre. In surface acting, you put on the expected expression without changing what you feel, smiling at a rude customer while seething inside. In deep acting, you try to summon the feeling itself, perhaps by reframing the customer as anxious rather than nasty, or by recalling something warm.\n\nLater researchers added a third case: sometimes workers simply feel what the job requires, such as real compassion for a patient, so no acting is needed. Hochschild also described feeling rules, the often unspoken norms about what we should feel in a situation, such as joy at a wedding or grief at a funeral."],
    ["The toll, and what helps", "Surface acting is linked to exhaustion and lower job satisfaction, while autonomy, support and genuine time off make emotional work more sustainable.",
      "Hochschild worried that long-term emotional labour could leave people unsure which feelings were really theirs. Researchers describe emotional dissonance: the gap between what you feel and what you must display. Pooled workplace studies, including a 2011 meta-analysis, link surface acting fairly consistently with emotional exhaustion, lower job satisfaction and more stress symptoms. Deep acting shows weaker, more mixed links with strain.\n\nEmotional labour is not inherently harmful, and many people find meaning in comforting others. It is easier to sustain with some freedom over how you express yourself, supportive colleagues and managers, and a workplace that backs staff against abusive customers. Genuinely switching off during time off also helps recovery."],
    ["Who does it", "Emotional labour is concentrated in service, care and frontline jobs, where lower-status workers are often expected to absorb rudeness.",
      "Hochschild estimated that about a third of American workers had jobs involving substantial emotional labour, and about half of working women, reflecting how many women worked in service and caring roles. Examples include retail and hospitality staff, call centre workers, nurses, teachers, childcare workers, paramedics, police and social workers.\n\nThe demands differ. Some roles require constant positivity, others composure amid distress or hostility, and corrections or security work means projecting calm authority while staying alert to danger. Status matters too: workers lower in the hierarchy are often expected to absorb rudeness without responding. Customer mistreatment is a significant stressor for frontline staff, especially when they cannot set limits."],
    ["A term that grew", "\"Emotional labour\" now often means unpaid relationship and household work, a stretch that Hochschild herself has questioned.",
      "In the 2010s the phrase escaped academic journals. People began using it for the unpaid work of remembering birthdays, organising family schedules, noticing what the household is running out of, and soothing everyone's moods. This load is often carried disproportionately by women. Related terms such as the mental load describe the invisible planning behind running a home.\n\nThese are real and important issues, and research on the division of household labour is extensive. But Hochschild has said in interviews that the term has sometimes been stretched to cover almost any chore, blurring her original focus on managing feelings, especially for a wage. When you meet the phrase, it helps to ask which meaning is intended."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Hochschild mean by emotional labour?", ["Doing physically tiring work for long hours without any breaks","Managing your own feelings at work so customers feel a certain way","Feeling stressed because of an unusually heavy workload","Listening to colleagues talk about their personal problems"], 1, "Hochschild showed that in many jobs, feelings become part of what the employer sells."],
    [1, "What is the difference between surface acting and deep acting?", ["Surface acting fakes the expression; deep acting tries to feel it","Surface acting is for customers; deep acting is for managers","Surface acting uses words only; deep acting uses body language","Surface acting is rehearsed; deep acting is always improvised"], 0, "Surface acting changes only what you show, while deep acting tries to change what you feel."],
    [2, "What does research link to regular surface acting?", ["Stronger friendships with workmates and customers","Faster career progress and higher pay","Better physical health and a steadier mood","Emotional exhaustion and lower job satisfaction"], 3, "Faking an emotion you do not feel takes effort, and the cost shows up as exhaustion and lower satisfaction."],
    [3, "Who is most likely to do heavy emotional labour at work?", ["Machine operators working alone on a factory floor","Warehouse staff who rarely deal with anyone else","Frontline and care workers, such as nurses and teachers","Software developers working on solo projects"], 2, "Emotional labour is concentrated in jobs that involve dealing directly with people's feelings."],
    [4, "Why does Hochschild question the modern use of \"emotional labour\"?", ["It was meant to describe only the work of flight attendants","It has been stretched to cover almost any chore","It now refers only to physical tasks done around the house","It was never meant to apply to paid work at all"], 1, "The original idea was about managing feelings, especially in paid work, so it helps to ask which meaning is intended."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Some jobs sell your smile. What does it cost the person behind it?"});

